{
  "primary_meaning": "The place or state of final divine punishment for the wicked after judgment (commonly equated with hell), often depicted with unquenchable fire and destruction of body and soul.",
  "primary_basis": [
    "Matthew 5:22, 5:29-30",
    "Matthew 10:28",
    "Matthew 18:9",
    "Matthew 23:15, 23:33",
    "Mark 9:43-48",
    "Luke 12:5",
    "James 3:6",
    "OT background of idolatrous child sacrifice and judgment in the Valley of Hinnom (e.g., 2 Kings 23:10; Jeremiah 7:31-32; 19:2-6)"
  ],
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "meaning": "Complete destruction/annihilation of the wicked (rather than eternal conscious torment), with Gehenna's fire consuming them entirely",
      "basis": [
        "Matthew 10:28 (destroy body and soul)",
        "Mark 9:43-48 imagery of consumption",
        "Broader biblical language of perishing and second death"
      ],
      "prominence": "common"
    },
    {
      "meaning": "Historical/eschatological judgment on Jerusalem (especially AD 70) or this-worldly ruin, using the literal Valley of Hinnom as a symbol of covenant curse rather than a postmortem realm",
      "basis": [
        "Jeremiah 7 and 19 prophecies against Judah",
        "Jesus' Olivet Discourse context and warnings to Israel",
        "Geographic reality of the valley outside Jerusalem"
      ],
      "prominence": "minority"
    },
    {
      "meaning": "Metaphor for severe shame, exclusion from God's kingdom, or the grave's corruption without specifying eternal torment",
      "basis": [
        "Jewish Second Temple usage of Gehenna imagery",
        "Hyperbolic rhetoric in the Sermon on the Mount",
        "Parallel warnings about entering life maimed vs. whole body cast out"
      ],
      "prominence": "minority"
    },
    {
      "meaning": "Temporary purifying punishment (closer to intermediate-state correction) rather than final irreversible hell",
      "basis": [
        "Some later Jewish traditions of limited Gehenna sentences",
        "Occasional patristic or modern readings distinguishing it from eternal fate"
      ],
      "prominence": "rare"
    }
  ],
  "has_settled_consensus": true,
  "confidence": "high",
  "notes": "Mainstream historic Christian teaching (across Catholic, Orthodox, and most Protestant traditions) consistently treats Gehenna as Jesus' primary term for final punitive hell, drawing on the valley's OT associations with abomination and fire. Debate exists over the nature and duration of that punishment, but the core identification with post-judgment retribution for the unrighteous is the recognizable consensus; purely non-eschatological or solely literal-geographic readings remain secondary."
}
